Opaque focus (dcsvr_two_layers_alpha=3 with dcsvr_two_layers; native/quadviews/dcsvr_twolayer_ps.hlsl and
dcsvr_opaque_focus.h): the runtime is never asked to alpha-blend. The focus layer goes out with no blend flags (neither
BLEND_TEXTURE_SOURCE_ALPHA nor UNPREMULTIPLIED_ALPHA), its view cut to the rectangle of the focus image where the
composition's fade is exactly 1 (imageRect and a field of view of that rectangle; the swapchain and the pass stay the
same, the pass writes alpha 1). The periphery always gets its own output pass, which draws the rest of the fade the way
the composition does at the periphery's pixels: the focus image (t3, the view the focus pass reads) over the periphery
with the composition's weights, edge match tent (k from the CPU, now computed before both passes) and Far depth
displacement. The two layers shown together give the composed image, at the focus resolution inside the rectangle
(tests/quadviews-composition). The rectangle depends only on the focus image's size, shape and smoothing area, so it
stays the same from frame to frame. Read at start only, one log line; 0, 1 and 2 are as before, byte for byte.
